00:00:14.821 --> 00:00:27.872
<v NARRATOR>In a warm bowl of flour and water, two old friends settled in to work in the dark, the way they had in sourdough for longer than anyone could remember.

4
00:00:27.872 --> 00:00:34.003
<v YEAST>Same bowl again, Lacto, and the same sugar for us both to share.

5
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:39.014
<v LACTO>Same bowl — and two completely different gifts to leave inside it.

6
00:00:39.014 --> 00:00:46.345
<v NARRATOR>A student leaned over the bowl, puzzled, and asked whether they were just doing the same job twice.

7
00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:56.356
<v LACTO>I ferment the sugar and my leftover is lactic acid, which makes the clean sour tang and keeps unwelcome microbes out.

8
00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:05.127
<v LACTO>But if you had only me, you would get a tangy, well-guarded dough that was flat and heavy as a stone.

9
00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:14.578
<v YEAST>Which is exactly where I come in — I ferment the very same sugar, but my leftover is carbon-dioxide gas.

10
00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:23.869
<v YEAST>My gas bubbles up and gets caught in the stretchy dough, thousands of little pockets, and the whole loaf swells and turns light.

11
00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:33.080
<v NARRATOR>Between the two of them — her tang and safety, his air and lift — the sourdough became something neither could make alone.

12
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:43.091
<v LACTO>We are both fermenters, cousins in the same family trick, but the leftover is the gift, and our gifts do not overlap.

13
00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:50.542
<v YEAST>The trap is thinking a partner has to be a copy of you — the best pairs are often the most different.

14
00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:56.873
<v NARRATOR>A student worried that she and a friend were too different to ever work well together.

15
00:01:56.873 --> 00:02:04.204
<v LACTO>Do not look for someone who does your job too; look for someone who does the job you cannot.

16
00:02:04.204 --> 00:02:15.055
<v NARRATOR>Come back in a few hours — the two of them are already in there, side by side, making something that needs them both. That is the rising.

